# Feature File Conventions

## File Organisation

- One feature file per module: `audience.feature`, `campaign.feature`, `workflow.feature`, etc.
- Smoke tests appear first, then regression scenarios below a comment separator
- Group related regression scenarios under comment headers

```gherkin
Feature: Module name

  @SmokeTest @ST-MODULE-01
  Scenario: Smoke test scenario
    ...

  # ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  # REGRESSION
  # ─────────────────────────────────────────────

  @Regression @Module @TC-MODULE-01
  Scenario: Regression test scenario
    ...
```

## Tagging Strategy

| Tag | Purpose | Used by runner |
|-----|---------|---------------|
| `@SmokeTest` | Core smoke tests | Main parallel run |
| `@Regression` | Extended regression tests | Filtered runs |
| `@TestPreparation` | Must run before parallel groups | Prerequisites phase |
| `@ExistingAudience` | Scenario creates a reusable published audience | Saves to `existingAudience` key |
| `@ST-<MODULE>-<NN>` | Smoke test identifier | Reporting |
| `@TC-<MODULE>-<NN>` | Regression test identifier | Reporting, `names.json` keys |

## Step Wording

- Steps must match existing step definitions exactly (case-sensitive)
- Check `src/steps/*.ts` before writing new step text
- Existing smoke test steps use lowercase verbs: `click the Set Goal button`
- Regression steps may use title case: `Click the Set Goal button`
- Do not mix styles within a single scenario
